  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "$\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{x}})=$",
  "worked_solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule and then the power rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3y}{\\sqrt{x}}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(3y)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
